# OTP manual page

.TH OTP 1 "2026-03-18" "OTP v1.1.0" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
otp \- simple one‑time pad encryption/decryption utility

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B otp
[options] <keyfile>
.br
.B otp
[-h | --help]
.br
.B otp
[-nk | --new-key-pair] <size_in_MB> <part_a_name> <part_b_name>
.br
.B otp
[-ac | --add-contact] <contact_name> [<encryption_key_file> <decryption_key_file>]
.br
.B otp
[-rc | --remove-contact] <contact_name>
.br
.B otp
[-hc | --has-contact] <contact_name>
.br
.B otp
[-lc | --list-contacts]
.br
.B otp
[-sc | --show-contact] <contact_name>
.br
.B otp
[-c | --contact] <contact_name> --encrypt
.br
.B otp
[-c | --contact] <contact_name> --decrypt

.SH DESCRIPTION
This program takes data from standard input, XORs it with a key file, and writes the result to standard output.  After processing it writes a new file containing the part of the key file that was not used, ending with ``.next``.  This ensures the key cannot be reused.  The key file must be a binary file containing random data; after each use the unused portion is moved to a new file for the next run. It also allows generating key pairs, manage a keychain of contacts and encrypt/decrypt using the keychain keys.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Display this help message and exit.

.TP
.B \-nk, \-\-new-key-pair
Generate a new key pair.  The program expects two key files for encryption and decryption.  The key material is read from standard input: the first half is written to an encryption file named ``encryption_<part_a_name>.txt``; the second half to ``decryption_<part_a_name>.txt``; and so on for ``part_b_name``.  The first argument is the size in megabytes (e.g., ``5`` for 5 MiB).  Example: cat /dev/urandom | otp --new-key-pair 5 partA partB

.TP
.B \-ac, \-\-add-contact <contact_name> [<encryption_key_file> <decryption_key_file>]
Add a new contact to the keychain.  Optionally specify encryption and decryption key files to load keys from disk (e.g., files generated by --new-key-pair).  The two key files must hold different key material and are compared by content: one pad serving both directions would mean the range encrypting an outgoing message also decrypts an incoming one, so an identical pair is refused.  Re-using a contact name that has been used on this keychain before produces a warning, because freshly supplying the original key from generation would restart over already-spent bytes.  If no key files are specified, the contact is created with empty keys.  Creates the contact's own metadata file (.keychain/<contact_name>.meta) with the new contact entry.  Each contact stores encryption/decryption keys, message sequence information, and retry counts.

.TP
.B \-rc, \-\-remove-contact <contact_name>
Remove a contact from the keychain.  Deletes the contact's metadata file and removes all associated data for the specified contact.

.TP
.B \-hc, \-\-has-contact <contact_name>
Check if a contact exists in the keychain.  Returns exit code 0 if the contact exists, 1 otherwise.

.TP
.B \-lc, \-\-list-contacts
List all contacts stored in the keychain.  Displays the names of all contacts found under the .keychain/ directory.

.TP
.B \-sc, \-\-show-contact <contact_name>
Show detailed information about a specific contact.  Displays all contact fields including encryption/decryption key sizes (keys themselves are masked for security), offsets, sequence numbers, timestamps, and retry counts.

.TP
.B \-c, \-\-contact <contact_name> \-\-encrypt
Encrypt data from stdin using the contact's encryption key and write the cipher text to stdout.  The encryption key is consumed and updated to contain only the remaining unused bytes.  Updates the contact's sequence number, encryption key offset, and sets the LastMessageSentAt timestamp.

.TP
.B \-c, \-\-contact <contact_name> \-\-decrypt
Decrypt data from stdin using the contact's decryption key and write the plain text to stdout.  The decryption key is consumed and updated to contain only the remaining unused bytes.  Updates the contact's sequence number, decryption key offset, and sets the LastMessageReceivedAt timestamp.

.SH USAGE
There are two ways to use this program: with key files directly, or with the keychain system.

.SS Using Key Files Directly
To encrypt using a key file:
.PP
.B echo "plain text" | otp key.txt > cipher.txt
.PP
To decrypt using a key file:
.PP
.B cat cipher.txt | otp key.txt > plain.txt
.PP
After each run, the file ``key.txt.next`` will contain the unused key bytes.

.SS Using the Keychain System
To encrypt using a contact's key from the keychain:
.PP
.B echo "plain text" | otp -c contact_name --encrypt > cipher.txt
.PP
To decrypt using a contact's key from the keychain:
.PP
.B cat cipher.txt | otp -c contact_name --decrypt > plain.txt
.PP
The keychain automatically manages key consumption and updates the contact's metadata. No .next files are created; the remaining key is stored in the keychain.

.SH NEXT KEY
Every time you run the command it will create a new file with the same name as the key file ending with ``.next``.

.SH NEW KEY PAIR GENERATION
Use the \fB-nk\fP or \fB--new-key-pair\fP flag to generate a new key pair from a source of randomness.
Example:
.B cat /dev/urandom | otp --new-key-pair 1 alice bob

.SH KEYCHAIN MANAGEMENT
The keychain feature allows you to manage contacts with their associated encryption and decryption keys.  Each contact's metadata and key files all live under the \fB.keychain/\fP subdirectory in the current directory.  The keychain provides a convenient way to perform one-time pad encryption and decryption with stored keys, automatically managing key consumption and metadata.

.PP
.B File Storage Architecture
.br
The keychain uses a streaming architecture that supports extremely large keys (up to 1 TB) without loading them into memory. Everything lives under \fB.keychain/\fP, one set of files per contact:
.IP \(bu 3
\fB<contact>.meta\fP \- that contact's metadata (key file paths, offsets, sequences, timestamps), written and committed independently of every other contact's file
.IP \(bu 3
\fB<contact>_enc.key\fP and \fB<contact>_dec.key\fP \- that contact's actual key files
.IP \(bu 3
\fB<contact>.lock\fP \- a per-contact lock file used to serialize concurrent operations on the same contact
.IP \(bu 3
Keys are read and processed in 4 MB chunks during encryption/decryption, enabling support for terabyte-sized keys without memory constraints
.IP \(bu 3
Everything otp creates inside \fB.keychain/\fP is created mode 0600, key files included
.IP \(bu 3
A contact's name is used verbatim as a filename, so it may not be \fB.\fP or \fB..\fP, contain a path separator, any of \fB: * ? " < > | =\fP, or control characters

.SS Contact Data Structure
Each contact in the keychain stores:
.PP
.B Name
\- The contact's name (unique identifier)
.br
.B EncryptionKey
\- Encryption key stored in .keychain/<contact>_enc.key (displayed as ******* with remaining bytes for security). This key is used when encrypting messages to send to the contact. The key file is read in streaming chunks during encryption.
.br
.B EncryptionKeyOffset
\- Number of bytes consumed from the encryption key file. This tracks cumulative consumption for reference purposes.
.br
.B EncryptedSequence
\- Sequence number incremented on every encryption operation. Tracks the total number of messages encrypted (sent) to this contact.
.br
.B DecryptionKey
\- Decryption key stored in .keychain/<contact>_dec.key (displayed as ******* with remaining bytes for security). This key is used when decrypting messages received from the contact. The key file is read in streaming chunks during decryption.
.br
.B DecryptionKeyOffset
\- Number of bytes consumed from the decryption key file. This tracks cumulative consumption for reference purposes.
.br
.B DecryptedSequence
\- Sequence number incremented on every decryption operation. Tracks the total number of messages decrypted (received) from this contact.
.br
.B RetryCount
\- Counter for message retry attempts (range: 0-25000). Can be used to track retransmission attempts.
.br
.B LastMessageSentAt
\- UTC timestamp of when the last message was encrypted and sent. Updated only during encryption operations.
.br
.B LastMessageReceivedAt
\- UTC timestamp of when the last message was decrypted and received. Updated only during decryption operations.
.PP

.SS How Keychain Encryption Works
The keychain-based encryption follows these steps:
.PP
.B 1. Key Pair Generation
.br
Generate matching key pairs for two parties (e.g., Alice and Bob) using --new-key-pair. This creates four files: encryption_alice.txt (Alice's encryption key = Bob's decryption key) and decryption_alice.txt (Alice's decryption key = Bob's encryption key), plus the corresponding pair for Bob.
.PP
.B 2. Add Contacts to Keychain
.br
Each party adds their contact with their respective keys to their keychain using --add-contact.
.PP
.B 3. Encrypt Messages
.br
When Alice encrypts a message using -c alice --encrypt, the program:
.RS
\- Reads the plaintext from stdin
.br
\- XORs it with Alice's encryption key (starting from the beginning of the remaining key)
.br
\- Writes the cipher text to stdout
.br
\- Increments the EncryptedSequence number
.br
\- Updates EncryptionKeyOffset by the message length
.br
\- Sets LastMessageSentAt to the current timestamp
.br
\- Removes the used key bytes and stores only the remaining unused portion
.br
\- Saves the updated keychain to disk
.RE
.PP
.B 4. Decrypt Messages
.br
When Bob decrypts the message using -c bob --decrypt, the program:
.RS
\- Reads the cipher text from stdin
.br
\- XORs it with Bob's decryption key (which matches Alice's encryption key)
.br
\- Writes the plaintext to stdout
.br
\- Increments the DecryptedSequence number
.br
\- Updates DecryptionKeyOffset by the message length
.br
\- Sets LastMessageReceivedAt to the current timestamp
.br
\- Removes the used key bytes and stores only the remaining unused portion
.br
\- Saves the updated keychain to disk
.RE
.PP
.B 5. Perfect Forward Secrecy
.br
Because keys are consumed and removed after each use (similar to the .next file mechanism), even if the keychain is compromised, previously encrypted messages cannot be decrypted. Each message uses a unique, never-reused portion of the one-time pad.
.PP

.SS Key Consumption Example
If a contact has a 1MB (1048576 bytes) encryption key and you encrypt a 100-byte message:
.RS
\- 100 bytes are used for XOR encryption
.br
\- The remaining 1048476 bytes are stored as the new encryption key
.br
\- EncryptionKeyOffset is updated from 0 to 100
.br
\- The next encryption will use bytes 100-199 of the original key
.RE
.PP

.SH KEYCHAIN EXAMPLES

.SS Basic Keychain Operations
To add a contact without keys:
.PP
.nf
.B otp --add-contact alice
.fi
.PP
To list all contacts:
.PP
.nf
.B otp --list-contacts
.fi
.PP
To show contact details:
.PP
.nf
.B otp --show-contact alice
.fi
.PP
To check if a contact exists:
.PP
.nf
.B otp --has-contact alice && echo "Contact exists"
.fi
.PP
To remove a contact:
.PP
.nf
.B otp --remove-contact alice
.fi
.PP

.SS Complete Workflow: Secure Communication Between Alice and Bob
This example demonstrates the complete workflow for setting up and using the keychain for secure communication between two parties.

.B Step 1: Generate Key Pairs (Run on a secure machine)
.PP
Generate a 1MB key pair for Alice and Bob. This creates four files with matching keys:
.PP
.nf
.B cat /dev/urandom | otp --new-key-pair 1 alice bob
.fi
.PP
This creates:
.RS
\- encryption_alice.txt (Alice uses to encrypt, Bob uses to decrypt)
.br
\- decryption_alice.txt (Alice uses to decrypt, Bob uses to encrypt)
.br
\- encryption_bob.txt (Bob uses to encrypt, Alice uses to decrypt)
.br
\- decryption_bob.txt (Bob uses to decrypt, Alice uses to encrypt)
.RE
.PP
Note: encryption_alice.txt and decryption_bob.txt contain identical keys, as do encryption_bob.txt and decryption_alice.txt.

.B Step 2: Distribute Keys Securely
.PP
Transfer the keys to Alice and Bob via a secure, out-of-band method (e.g., encrypted USB drive, in-person exchange):
.RS
\- Alice receives: encryption_alice.txt and decryption_alice.txt
.br
\- Bob receives: encryption_bob.txt and decryption_bob.txt
.RE
.PP

.B Step 3: Alice Adds Bob to Her Keychain
.PP
On Alice's machine:
.PP
.nf
.B otp --add-contact bob encryption_alice.txt decryption_alice.txt
.fi
.PP
Output:
.RS
Contact 'bob' added with keys:
.br
  Encryption key: 1048576 bytes from encryption_alice.txt
.br
  Decryption key: 1048576 bytes from decryption_alice.txt
.RE
.PP

.B Step 4: Bob Adds Alice to His Keychain
.PP
On Bob's machine:
.PP
.nf
.B otp --add-contact alice encryption_bob.txt decryption_bob.txt
.fi
.PP
Output:
.RS
Contact 'alice' added with keys:
.br
  Encryption key: 1048576 bytes from encryption_bob.txt
.br
  Decryption key: 1048576 bytes from decryption_bob.txt
.RE
.PP

.B Step 5: Alice Sends an Encrypted Message to Bob
.PP
On Alice's machine:
.PP
.nf
.B echo "Hello Bob! This is a secret message." | otp -c bob --encrypt > message1.bin
.fi
.PP
Stderr output:
.RS
Used 38 bytes from encryption key for contact 'bob'
.br
Remaining encryption key: 1048538 bytes
.RE
.PP
The file message1.bin contains the cipher text. Alice sends this file to Bob through any channel (email, file transfer, etc.) since it is encrypted.

.B Step 6: Bob Decrypts Alice's Message
.PP
Bob receives message1.bin and decrypts it on his machine:
.PP
.nf
.B cat message1.bin | otp -c alice --decrypt
.fi
.PP
Output to stdout:
.RS
Hello Bob! This is a secret message.
.RE
.PP
Stderr output:
.RS
Used 38 bytes from decryption key for contact 'alice'
.br
Remaining decryption key: 1048538 bytes
.RE
.PP

.B Step 7: Bob Sends an Encrypted Reply to Alice
.PP
On Bob's machine:
.PP
.nf
.B echo "Hi Alice! Got your message." | otp -c alice --encrypt > reply1.bin
.fi
.PP
Stderr output:
.RS
Used 27 bytes from encryption key for contact 'alice'
.br
Remaining encryption key: 1048549 bytes
.RE
.PP

.B Step 8: Alice Decrypts Bob's Reply
.PP
Alice receives reply1.bin and decrypts it:
.PP
.nf
.B cat reply1.bin | otp -c bob --decrypt
.fi
.PP
Output to stdout:
.RS
Hi Alice! Got your message.
.RE
.PP
Stderr output:
.RS
Used 27 bytes from decryption key for contact 'bob'
.br
Remaining decryption key: 1048549 bytes
.RE
.PP

.B Step 9: Verify Contact Status
.PP
Alice can check her contact status at any time:
.PP
.nf
.B otp --show-contact bob
.fi
.PP
Output shows:
.RS
Contact: bob
.br
  EncryptionKey: ******* (1048538 bytes)
.br
  EncryptionKeyOffset: 38
.br
  EncryptedSequence: 1
.br
  DecryptionKey: ******* (1048549 bytes)
.br
  DecryptionKeyOffset: 27
.br
  DecryptedSequence: 1
.br
  RetryCount: 0
.br
  LastMessageSentAt: [timestamp]
.br
  LastMessageReceivedAt: [timestamp]
.RE
.PP
The two sequence counters are tracked separately, one per direction: Alice has sent 1 message and received 1.

.SS Important Security Notes
.RS
\- Never reuse keys. Once keys are exhausted, generate new key pairs.
.br
\- The .keychain/ directory and everything in it should be kept secure with appropriate file permissions (700 for the .keychain/ directory, 600 for the files inside it).
.br
\- Use a true hardware random number generator (TRNG) for key generation in production.
.br
\- Exchange key files through secure, out-of-band channels.
.br
\- Monitor key consumption using --show-contact to know when keys are running low.
.br
\- Back up your .keychain/ directory securely if you need to preserve keys and message history.
.br
\- The streaming architecture allows keys up to 1 TB in size, enabling extremely long-term secure communication without memory constraints.
.RE
.PP

.SH EXIT STATUS
For keychain encrypt/decrypt operations
.RB ( \-c ):
.TP
.B 0
Success \- the input was encrypted or decrypted and delivered.
.TP
.B 3
A message from an interrupted earlier run was recovered and redelivered, and
.B this invocation's input was not processed.
The output is valid, but it belongs to the earlier message. Run the command again to process the input that was skipped.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred; nothing was delivered and no key material was consumed.
.PP
All other keychain commands return 0 on success and 1 on failure, except
.B \-\-has-contact,
which returns 0 if the contact exists and 1 if it does not.

.SH TRUE RANDOM KEY GENERATOR
Only a true random key makes this algorithm unbreakable. To generate a true random key consider
.B https://www.crowdsupply.com/leetronics/infinite-noise-trng

